\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The norm pairing and the integral alternating pairing}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{In the ordered Hurwitz basis of \ref{fdip-00V1},
the rational norm pairing has Gram matrix
##{G_0=\begin{pmatrix}
1&0&0&1/2\\0&1&0&1/2\\0&0&1&1/2\\1/2&1/2&1/2&1
\end{pmatrix}.}
Subtracting half of each of the first three rows from the
last row gives #{\det G_0=1/4}. The form with matrix #{2G_0}
is integral but has determinant #{2^4/4=4}. Thus neither
#{G_0} nor #{2G_0} is a perfect integral symmetric pairing
on this lattice.}

\p{Evaluating the [integral alternating form](fdip-00V2)
on the same basis gives
##{[b_{\mathbb Z}]=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&-1&-1&-1\\
1&0&0&0\\
1&0&0&1\\
1&0&-1&0
\end{pmatrix}.}
For example, #{h(\qi +\qj)=\qj -1} gives its last
column #{(-1,0,1,0)^{\mathsf T}}. The rank-#{4} formula
of \ref{fdip-00US} gives
##{\pfaffian([b_{\mathbb Z}])
 =(-1)(1)-(-1)(0)+(-1)(0)=-1.}
Its determinant is one by \ref{fdip-00UT}. The two pairing
choices therefore have different integral properties, even
though the rational norm pairing is positive definite.}
